Verification date: 2026-08-13 (Europe/Moscow)

Environment
-----------
Python 3.12.13
MuJoCo 3.11.0
MediaPipe 0.10.35
OpenCV 5.0.0
websocket-client 1.9.0

Automated checks
----------------
python -m compileall -q src tests tools: PASS
python -m pytest -q: 191 passed in 96.76s
git diff --check: PASS

Grounded physics and motor-control checks
-----------------------------------------
Both foot collision geoms contact the ground in fixed and free scenes.
Grounded-fixed normal force after settling: about 28.8 N.
Robot source mass: 2.933134 kg; expected weight at 9.81 m/s^2: 28.77 N.
Visual soles and collision soles remain within about 1 mm of the ground.
No non-ground proxy self-contact is present in the home pose.

The free-base controller writes only the canonical 20 motor position targets.
It never writes the floating-base qpos, creates a weld, or applies external force.
The compiled acceptance model has neq=0 and a real base_free joint. A sagittal
capture-point governor uses CoM/velocity and measured sole loads, while support
transitions are gated by load, touchdown, base tilt and angular speed. The live,
slew-limited capture residual remains active while camera leg targets are frozen
during a support cycle. Opposite-leg requests use a bounded one-slot queue and
are executed only after returning through DOUBLE_SUPPORT.

Coordinate/retargeting checks
-----------------------------
MediaPipe camera axes are treated as +X image-right, +Y image-down, +Z away.
Robot physical front is MuJoCo -X; robot left is +Y; robot up is +Z.
Semantic FK tests verify that a human right-arm/right-leg forward motion moves
only the matching robot limb toward physical robot front, rather than merely
checking a motor-array sign. Angular deltas use shortest-path wrapping at +/-pi.
The Unity FRONT camera/front_motor and converted long-toe geometry independently
confirm the -X robot-front convention. Tasks hidden during explicit neutral
calibration remain unavailable until recalibration; a moving pose cannot become
a lazy per-task zero.

Visual checks
-------------
21 Unity FBX links converted to 21 visual-only Wavefront OBJ meshes.
Meshes: 259,480 render vertices; 122,251 triangles.
Both MJCF scenes compile with separate render groups:
  group 0 = environment
  group 1 = visual OBJ meshes
  group 2 = physical collision proxies
Offscreen visual and joints views rendered successfully. A synchronized
human/MediaPipe versus actual grounded-fixed MuJoCo contact sheet was generated
as artifacts/ik-pose-comparison.png. Raw IK fidelity over the bundled clip and
four external public-domain clips is stored in artifacts/pose-fidelity.json.

End-to-end checks
-----------------
The reproducible six-video report is artifacts/freebase-stability.json. Every
clip passed every gate with the exact same criteria: base mode free, neq=0,
base_free joint type, one HumanoidSimulation and one MjData through input and
settling, zero falls, zero non-foot ground contacts, bounded stance-foot slip,
material copied motion, and five quiet seconds after the final frame.

Bundled slow-balance MP4, full file plus same-simulation settling:
frames=1961, skeleton=1960, stale=1, fell=0, min/final base z=0.8422/0.9292 m,
max tilt=14.576 deg, right/left clearance=0.0366/0.0316 m, transitions=21,
settling=5.000 s, media sync error=0.00073 s.

Other matrix clips, maximum tilt (all fell=0 and settled 5.000 s):
jumping jacks 3.943 deg; arm circles 6.947 deg; frontal leg swing 11.719 deg;
stationary squat 6.630 deg; trunk circles 2.482 deg.

Worst load-bearing stance-foot slip speed across the matrix was 0.131 m/s;
accumulated slip for every foot stayed below 0.061 m. Swing-foot collision
impulses are logged separately instead of being mislabeled as stance slip.
Runtime input SHA-256 values were unchanged for the entire final matrix run.

The automated WebSocket regression confirms that an unreachable endpoint is
isolated from camera/physics completion and receives the same post-controller
safe command rather than the raw human target.

Hardware limitation
-------------------
No Windows camera device was visible in this tool session, so a physical webcam
frame could not be verified. The bundled MediaPipe Full model and full MP4 path
were executed through the real Tasks API. Real-robot feedback is not yet wired:
the optional legacy WebSocket output is an experimental/shadow motor-command
boundary and must not be powered without encoder/IMU/foot feedback, onboard slew
limits/watchdog and a physical E-stop.
